{#
    `.. versionadded::`, `.. versionchanged::`, `.. deprecated::`.

    **This is a note, and not nearly as loosely as it sounds.** `sds-note` says
    of itself: a title that states the fact, and a bounded line under it that
    says what the fact costs the reader. A version change is that
    sentence twice over. "Changed in version 1.2" is the fact, and the
    paragraph under it is what the change costs somebody who reads the page
    today. Nothing new is necessary to say it. A vocabulary that grows a
    second label-over-prose block has two of them that disagree within a
    release.

    **Only deprecation carries a tone.** `warn` in this system is the degraded
    but usable answer, which is exactly what a deprecated thing is. It still
    works, and it is on its way out. The other two are facts about the surface
    and nothing has gone wrong in either. So they are `info`, and `info` is
    the tone that does not tint.

    That matters on a reference page, where an API with a long history
    carries one of these every third paragraph. Tint them all and the page
    reads as an alarm about itself. That is the reason `sds-note` already
    gives for `warn` as the only filled tone.

    The label the glyph says out loud stays the tone's own. The heading is
    visible and already names the version, so a truer word here only reads
    out twice.
#}
{%- set tone = node.type == 'deprecated' ? 'warn' : 'info' -%}
{#- `versionLabel` arrives formatted — the node has already put the version
    into "New in version %s". It is text, which is what a heading takes. -#}
<sds-note tone="{{ tone }}" heading="{{ node.versionLabel }}">{{ renderNode(node.value) }}</sds-note>
